lesson.body:
In the last section, computer science was described as the study of turning problems into instructions a machine can follow. Computational thinking is the name for the mental toolkit that makes that turn possible. It is not a programming language and it is not tied to any particular device. It is a way of approaching a problem, any problem, so that a solution can eventually be expressed as a precise, step by step procedure. You can practice computational thinking with a pencil and paper, long before you write a single line of Python.

Computational thinking is usually broken into four interlocking strategies. Decomposition means breaking a large, intimidating problem into smaller pieces that are easier to handle individually. Pattern recognition means noticing similarities between the current problem and problems you have solved before, or noticing repetition within the problem itself. Abstraction means deciding which details matter for solving the problem and which details can be safely ignored. Algorithm design means assembling a precise sequence of steps, using the smaller pieces and patterns you found, that actually produces the answer. Each of these gets its own full treatment later in this course, but they only make sense together, which is why computational thinking comes first.

Consider planning a road trip across several states. You would not try to plan the entire route in one mental leap. Instead, you decompose the trip into legs between cities, recognize that each leg is really the same subproblem (find a route from point A to point B), abstract away irrelevant details like the color of the cars you will pass, and focus on what matters: distance, road conditions, and rest stops. Finally, you assemble an ordered plan: drive to city one, refuel, drive to city two, and so on. That entire process, before any car engine starts, is computational thinking.

The same pattern shows up constantly in code. Suppose a program needs to check whether every number in a list is positive. A computational thinker does not stare at the list and guess. They decompose the task into "check one number" and "repeat for every number," recognize that "check one number" is a simple comparison, abstract away everything about the list except its individual values, and then write the algorithm:

def all_positive(numbers):
    for n in numbers:
        if n <= 0:
            return False
    return True

Tracing this with [5, 12, 8, 3] never triggers the n <= 0 condition, so the loop finishes and the function returns True. Tracing it with [5, -2, 8] triggers that condition on the second value, and the function returns False immediately without even looking at 8. Notice how the four strategies show up directly in the code's structure: the loop is decomposition (one number at a time), the condition n <= 0 is pattern recognition applied to what "not positive" means, and the whole function is an abstraction that hides this logic behind a simple name you can reuse anywhere. The rest of this unit studies each of these four strategies on its own.
